>
  <p>If you need to cool one room, there are three common refrigerated air-conditioning options worth considering: a portable unit, a window/wall unit or a split system.</p>
  <p>All can cool a room, but they solve the installation problem in very different ways.</p>
  <p>The choice depends on installation permission, expected use, noise, efficiency and the available window, wall and outdoor space.</p>
  <p>For the wider system, climate and installation questions, start with the <a href="/guides/air-conditioning-buying-guide/">Air Conditioning Buying Guide</a>.</p>

  <h2>Quick comparison</h2>
  <p class="table-hint">Swipe sideways to compare the full table.</p>
  <div class="comparison-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Portable, window and split-system air-conditioner comparison">
    <table class="comparison-table">
      <caption>Common differences between three single-room air-conditioner formats</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Type</th>
          <th scope="col">Main advantage</th>
          <th scope="col">Main drawback</th>
          <th scope="col">Often suits</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Portable</th>
          <td>No permanent installation</td>
          <td>Typically less efficient and noisier than many fixed systems</td>
          <td>Renters, temporary or occasional cooling</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Window/wall</th>
          <td>Hot side sits outside without a flexible exhaust hose</td>
          <td>Requires a suitable, supported window or wall opening</td>
          <td>Small rooms, existing openings, simpler permanent cooling</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Split system</th>
